\chapter{Hygienic expression macros}
\label{ch:38}
\index{Hygienic expression macros}

Source macros transform syntax before ordinary inference. Hygienic expansion preserves the origin of names written by the caller and distinguishes names introduced by the transformer. If a macro creates a temporary with a common spelling, a caller's same-spelled local must stay distinct. Expansion owns budgets for recursion and copied data, and the resulting program still meets ordinary type and ownership rules.

\section{Exercise a capture hazard}
Take an expression macro from the examples and expand it in a scope containing a local with the same spelling as an introduced binder. Make the values distinguishable and inspect the expanded AST with \OnxCodeTerm{--emit-ast}. Use an ill-typed operand to verify that correct expansion still leaves a normal type error. During diagnosis, separate a mismatch in macro input shape from a type failure in the expansion and from an unsupported PPX extension. The first is governed by macro pattern rules, described in \cref{ch:39}.

\section{Account for each occurrence of a name}
In \OnxCodeTerm{bind\_once}, distinguish three pieces supplied by the caller: an identifier for the result binding, an expression to evaluate, and a block in which the result will be used. The transformer's private binding, by contrast, is literally written into its template. Imagine the caller has already bound \OnxCodeTerm{temporary} to 99 and invokes the rule as follows:
\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none]
bind_once!(answer, temporary + 1, {
  println(answer);
  println(temporary);
});
\end{OnxCodeBlock}
Trace the names with pencil marks for origin. The use inside the addition is from the caller and reads 99. The binding introduced by the template receives 100. The template's subsequent read of its private binding refers to that same new binding; its value initializes the caller-supplied identifier. In the block, the first print uses that identifier and prints 100. The second reads the caller's original variable and prints 99. Unconditionally replacing every occurrence of the spelling by a fresh name would be just as wrong as replacing none.

Hygiene alone does not explain why the addition is evaluated once. Count how many times the template inserts the expression capture: once, as the initializer. If a different transformer inserted it twice in two evaluated positions, even perfect binding hygiene would not prevent two evaluations. This matters when the argument computes its value by calling a function with a visible effect. Test the count with a marker print, independently of testing which binding the expanded body reads.

